content marketing strategy by the end of  this lesson you will be able to outline  steps needed to create a content  strategy describe the importance of  various parts of a content marketing  plan including personas brand story  listening and measurement choose the  right channels that will complement your  content and prioritize and plan content  ideas around your company's biggest  opportunities content strategy versus  content marketing strategy there is a  difference between content strategy and  content marketing where content strategy  focus on the actual content content  marketing focuses on the outcome content  strategy is about a management and  creation of the media the tools you are  using the resources you have available  what are the types of content you will  be creating where content marketing is  all about the customer who are you  targeting with the content how well do  you know this person and most important  of all what is the expected business  outcome are you creating and sharing  content to build brand awareness or are  you aiming to increase revenue more  generic content marketing questions are  how it fits within your overall digital  marketing strategy what does your  content calendar look like and also by  sharing your content are you exposed to  risk in any way does your content work  for every market  content creation framework a good way to  start putting together your content  marketing strategy is by creating your  content marketing framework it enables  you to map out the required content  along the customer buying journey while  considering the impact it has on your  online presence your social media your  website and your sales between these two  areas the customer journey and your  overall digital marketing approach you  have two three types of content you need  to focus on linked content conversion  content and authority content link  content is content that drives traffic  from the internet and from your social  media to your website this is content  that is created with the goal of  attracting quality backlinks for SEO  purposes conversion content is content  that drives web visitors to the next  stage in the buying cycle through call  to actions or CTAs this is the content  that is created with the goal to  convince prospects that they should buy  from you  authority content drives web visitors to  sales through call to actions and drives  repeat traffic to your website this is  content that is created to build a  community of people that trust you and  see you as the expert in your fields  this is a useful structure to organize  your content and it keeps your focus on  the customer needs and the impact on  your overall digital marketing  objectives each type of content has  different requirements link content  needs to be high quality and often based  on research so other websites can refer  to it it's all geared towards generating  high quality backlinks to your website  which Google sees is very important for  your search ranking make this content  fail-proof repurpose it and promote it  like crazy conversion content is based  on helping the customer to go through  the buying cycle this will only work if  you understand what they need and that  your content clearly articulates that  you can help them solve their problem  but on top of that it is vital in this  stage that your content gives them the  option to move to the next stage through  very clear and compelling call to  actions and authority content is a  logical follow up from your link content  it is content that confirms you are an  expert in your fields it helps you to  build trust as long as you are truthful  transparent and show integrity and done  continuously and consistently it helps  you to deepen the relationship with your  prospects and your clients content  marketing strategy  there are various steps in developing  your content marketing strategy the  obvious thing of course is to start with  setting your objectives but additionally  you need to do a number of things  creates customer personas create  listening outposts write your brand  story develop your channel strategy  create your content marketing playbook  measure your content marketing and  establish your content marketing ROI  let's look at these elements of your  content marketing strategy in a bit more  detail customer personas a vital step in  your content marketing strategy is  creating customer personas this is a  concept that comes from the user  interface and design world in a customer  persona you look at things like  demographic characteristics like age and  gender but it is important to add to  this what their pain points are what is  their affinity with technology where do  they hang out online and what are their  interests and their hobbies once you  completed your customer personas print  them out and put them on the wall in  your office and make sure you add them  to your briefing document when you ask  your agency to create a new campaign  HubSpot has a great free tool to create  customer personas by asking a set of  questions it will put it in a format for  you that you can share and print and you  can tailor it to your own requirements  and add your own sections it includes  things like education social networks  they use preferred method of  communication tools they need  to do their job goals and objectives  challenges and how they gain knowledge  establish listening post an important  part of your content marketing strategy  is to listen to what people are talking  about online you can find out what they  think about your brand the topics they  talk about and if there are any issues  it is also great to listen online to  find good content you could share you  don't have to share just your own  content there's nothing wrong with  sharing relevant content from others  obviously when I say listening online I  mean this in the widest sense watching  videos reading articles or blogs looking  at photos or infographics or literally  listening to podcasts here are a few  tools you can use to listen feedly is a  reader app on which you can listen to  life RSS feeds  Google Alerts and talk Walker are tools  that will send you a notification for  certain terms that you have set you can  use Google Trends to find out search  trends for keywords your brand's story  the next thing you need to work out for  your content marketing strategy is your  brand story what is your mission your  vision your values what is the story of  your company your CEO remember people  want to listen and share stories when I  searched for IKEA on Google you get an  understanding of what I mean stories  about the history of the company the  founder of the company the way they work  the employees what makes them unique and  the more twists turns and uncertainties  in these stories the more people will  love them and it gets even better if you  can show them how you overcame obstacles  and bumps in the road this will increase  your credibility a good example of this  is Elon Musk from Tesla he came from a  very simple background but kept on  following his beliefs even when he was  nearly bankrupt he kept on going and  look where he is now  an important element of defining your  content marketing  strategy is understanding of what kind  of branch you are the type of branch you  are determines the type of social media  you will get with your content if you  are a basic brand you won't get many  people to talk about you let alone  comment on your social media posts  you're just fulfilling a basic needs the  only way these brands can get engagement  on social media  is to turn themselves into a funny brand  like Blendtec blenders who launched the  will it blend campaign if you are a  functional brand you will have customers  who ask questions about how to use your  product you need to respond to that by  offering content in which you answer  their questions exciting brands don't  have a problem to get social media  engagement with their content they have  thousands of fans who love to talk about  the brand's Harley Davidson is a classic  example these brands need to keep on  posting amazing content that become  almost like collector items for their  fans finally vital brands are for the  greater benefit of mankind  Greenpeace World Wildlife Fund UNICEF  they have no problem in getting  engagement and discussions but they  should be very alert on what people are  talking about and try to find  influencers that can help them spread  their course so think about is when you  start putting your content marketing  strategy together it will guide you on  the type of content and campaigns you  should create and the types of calls to  action you should set social channel  strategy before you're going to decide  on the type of content you're going to  create it makes sense to agree on the  social media channels you are going to  use in the listening stage you have  learned on which channels your clients  hangout so now you're going to make sure  you are there as well you're going to  synchronize the branding the key  messages and the campaigns to grow your  followers on these channels you need to  make sure you understand the latest  features and trends on these social  channels it is also good to do  content gap analysis what type of  content do you already have for each  channel and where are the gaps so you  know what type of content you need to  create here is a useful template you can  use for this it shows you the channels  to structure the tone of voice for each  Channel and to desired action the gaps  will give you an indication of the work  you need to do to make sure you have a  coherent content marketing strategy  content marketing playbook so now you  are in a position to decide on the type  of content you're going to create here  is a list structured by the three types  of content we saw earlier link  conversion and authority content link  content and authority content can be  fairly similar its moral the promotion  site where they differ but the  conversion content needs to be organized  along the customer journey from  awareness to evaluation to purchase we  call this the marketing playbook  you need to create your own by selecting  the right content for the right stage  measuring content marketing efforts you  can measure the traffic you get through  your content you can measure the  engagement you get and you can measure  the sentiments whether you were  targeting consumers or businesses the  metrics are the same but the priorities  may differ today lead generation is seen  as the number one KPI when it comes to  measuring the success of your content  marketing closely followed by traffic  generation to your website these metrics  show how your content marketing efforts  are succeeding but you need to go a  level deeper to discover what specific  content campaigns channels and efforts  are most effective in driving value and  in the end whether you are in b2c or b2b  it comes down to measuring the ROI of  your content marketing how much money  did you earn from your content marketing  in relation to the cost you incurred in  creating and distributing it the things  that can improve your ROI or quality of  your leads sales wept  fake engagement SEO success and brand  exposure and remember improved ROI is  not just based on more sales  it can also be based on lowering costs  planning your content marketing strategy  planning your content marketing strategy  is very important there are so many  things you need to take into  consideration if you don't put some  structure into this you may lose control  it is best to create your content  marketing process guides in this guide  you specify how you create and manage  your content how you optimize and curate  how you listen and engage in  conversations and how you measure your  content marketing besides this there is  the tactical stuff like roles and  responsibilities what are the style and  writing guidelines and similar what are  the guidelines for posting and  publishing and make sure you communicate  a process guide to the relevant people  in your organization you may want to put  a training together to the people who  will work with this on a daily basis a  vital part of your process guide is the  content marketing calendar it's the  calendar that contains your content  production and publication schedule  there are many templates and tools for  this on the internet my favorite ones  are Trello comm and Google Calendar the  most important part of it is that it is  a collaborative tool so you can schedule  the calendar with your colleagues or  with your clients you can give people  viewing or editing access you can use  color codes for the different types of  content or for the different channels  becoming a content marketing company to  give you a framework on how to organize  your content marketing strategy in your  company you can look at the Content  Marketing Institute for business models  for content marketing they can be mapped  in a quadrant with strategic importance  on one axis and internal external focus  on the other you're a player when your  content marketing strategy is focused on  supporting teams within the organization  like your product marketing or marketing  communication teams  most companies start a content marketing  like this it's seen as a contributor  you're a performer when you're content  marketing is seen as a center of  excellence in your organization it has  an external focus where audience  segmentation is key it can be seen as a  support function to other parts in the  organization you're a processor when you  centralize certain content functions but  not the actual creation of it your  corporate team may create a content  campaigns with the corporate guidelines  where your local teams are responsible  for local content creation to execute a  campaign and your platform when you  approach a content marketing as a  strategic business within your business  often this is done on a corporate level  and this is separate from local content  marketing initiatives so you need to  understand where you are today and have  a vision of where you want to go so here  are the key takeaways from this lesson  content strategy is the management of  any tangible media written visual or  audible that you create and own content  marketing is about creating and  distributing valuable relevant and  consistent content to generate website  traffic and new leads the content  creation framework consists of linked  content conversion content and authority  content it puts your content in the  context of the customer journey a  customer persona is a semi-fictional  representation of your ideal customer  based on market research and real data  about your existing customers stories  are the currency of content marketing  and your story starts with your mission  and your editorial statements and  content marketing ROI is how much  revenue you came from your content  marketing in comparison to what you  spent on creating and distributing your  content  [Music]
 

